{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-feed `refresh_interval` (integer minutes, 0 = use the global interval). The build and existing tests pass (53 unit packages, 102 integration tests); the new behaviour itself is not covered by any test I ran.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **Migration**: new `feeds.refresh_interval int not null default 0` column.\n- **API**: the field appears in the feed object and is accepted by `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}`; a negative value is rejected with a 400. The Go `client` package has the field too (`int` on `Feed`, `*int` on the modification request).\n- **Web UI**: the feed edit form has a number input named `refresh_interval`; a negative or non-numeric value is saved as 0.\n- **Scheduler**: `Feed.ScheduleNextCheck` uses the feed's interval when it is above 0, instead of the global round-robin / entry-frequency interval and its min/max limits.\n\nBehaviours you might not assume:\n- **Server delays still win when longer.** A Retry-After, TTL, Cache-Control or Expires delay longer than the feed's interval is still honoured, so a feed set to 5 minutes with a 60-minute TTL is checked every 60 minutes. I read \"overrides the global one\" as replacing only the global interval; if the per-feed value should be exact, it is a one-line change in `ScheduleNextCheck`.\n- **Changing the interval can bring the next check forward.** When a new non-zero interval is saved and the feed's next check is further away than that interval, the next check is moved up to now + interval. Otherwise a shortened interval would not take effect until the old schedule fired.\n- **Not settable at creation.** The field is not accepted on feed creation or carried through OPML import/export; new feeds start at 0.\n- **Translations are English placeholders.** The two new strings (form label and validation error) were added to all 24 locale files in English, because the locale test requires every key in every language.", "t": 1794461684.577}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794461684.577}
